Z||000000||zebrex||03-12-2000||02:25 AM||||I just bought my first fiero from my boss for 300 bucks. it is a 88 formula in really good shape except for a somewhat faded paint job and the rear brakes are shot. My question is what is the trick to cranking the callipers in? I bought the funky tool but they still dont compress completly. Im a fiero newbee any help is greatly welcome.<p>[This message has been edited by zebrex (edited 03-12-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||03-12-2000||11:52 AM||||The rear calipers have a screw with a one way clutch going through the middle of the caliper piston.  This allows the emergency brake to work, but the pistons now must be screwed in and out.  Inline force will break them.  Really kinda need a book for first time repair, although it can be puzzled out. The screw in piston is the only real surprise.||||reg||12
Z||000002||zebrex||03-12-2000||07:28 PM||||Thanks for the help I finally got frustated enough to spend $75 per calliper at the zone. Mine would only screw in so far then stop I dont know if the screw was stripped or what . I got the new ones home assembled them and installed them on the car things were going great execpt for the small problem of the reman company putting a helicoil in one of the banjo bolt holes. needless to say I took it back. my new one should be here tomorrow, hopefully with no helicoil in it.||||reg||1
